    "content": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite a LinkedIn post of up to 500 words about a lesson learned from shipping a feature that nobody used.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nWe once spent six weeks building a feature customers had asked for.\n\nThe request appeared in interviews, support tickets, and sales calls. It seemed obvious: people wanted more control, so we built a flexible set of options, added permissions, polished the interface, and announced the release.\n\nAlmost nobody used it.\n\nAt first, we blamed discoverability. We added tooltips, emails, and in-app prompts. Usage barely moved.\n\nThe real problem was simpler. We had listened to what customers said they wanted, but we had not understood the job they were trying to do. They did not actually want more control. They wanted to feel confident that the default behavior would work without creating problems.\n\nWe had built a better steering wheel for people who wanted a reliable train.\n\nThat experience changed how I think about customer requests. A request is evidence, not a specification. It points to friction, but it does not always identify the right solution.\n\nNow, before committing to a feature, I try to ask a few more questions. What happened right before someone asked for this? What are they doing today instead? How often does the problem occur? What would improve if we solved it? Can we test the behavior with a manual process or a rough prototype first?\n\nShipping something nobody uses is painful, but it can be valuable if it improves the way you build.\n\nThe lesson was not to ignore customers. It was to listen more carefully, test assumptions earlier, and measure success by changed behavior rather than shipped code.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nWe shipped a feature nobody used.\n\nThe team had spent weeks designing it, building it, polishing edge cases, and preparing the launch. Internally, everyone agreed it was valuable.\n\nCustomers disagreed—mostly by ignoring it.\n\nAt first, we blamed discoverability. We changed the placement, added tooltips, and mentioned it in onboarding.\n\nUsage barely moved.\n\nThe uncomfortable truth: we had validated the problem, but not the urgency.\n\nCustomers agreed the feature sounded useful. But “useful” wasn’t enough to change their behavior. The problem wasn’t frequent, painful, or important enough to earn a place in their workflow.\n\nThat experience changed how I think about product development.\n\nBefore investing heavily, I now try to answer:\n\n- When did the customer last experience this problem?\n- What are they doing today to solve it?\n- What does that workaround cost them?\n- Would they change their behavior—or pay—to make the problem disappear?\n- What is the smallest experiment that could test real demand?\n\nPositive feedback can be misleading. People are generous with opinions, especially when saying yes costs nothing.\n\nBehavior is stronger evidence.\n\nA waitlist is stronger than enthusiasm.  \nA prototype test is stronger than a survey.  \nA manual pilot is stronger than a feature request.  \nRepeated use is stronger than all of them.\n\nThe feature wasn’t a complete failure. It gave us something more valuable than adoption: a better standard for deciding what to build.\n\nDon’t just validate that a problem exists.\n\nValidate that it matters enough for someone to act.\n\n#ProductManagement #ProductDevelopment #Startups #CustomerDiscovery #LessonsLearned\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
